In the Cartesian coordinate system $xOy$, the curve $C$ is given by $\begin{cases} x=\sqrt{3}\cos \alpha, \\ y=\sin \alpha \end{cases}$ (with $\alpha$ as the parameter). In the polar coordinate system with the origin $O$ as the pole and the non-negative half-axis of $x$ as the polar axis, the polar equation of line $l$ is $\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\rho \cos \left( \theta +\frac{\pi }{4} \right)=-1$.

$(1)$ Find the general equation of curve $C$ and the Cartesian equation of line $l$;

$(2)$ A line $l_1$ passing through point $M(-1,0)$ and parallel to line $l$ intersects curve $C$ at points $A$ and $B$. Find the product of the distances from point $M$ to points $A$ and $B$.

